<given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><source><italic>Brain of the Firm</italic></source><year>1981</year><edition>2nd edition</edition><publisher-loc>New York, NY, USA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>John Wiley &#x00026; Sons</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B1"><label>7</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Boyd</surname><given-names>NF</given-names></name><name><surname>Wolfson</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Moskowitz</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Observer variation in the classification of mammographic parenchymal patterns</article-title><source><italic>Journal of Chronic Diseases</italic></source><year>1986</year><volume>39</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>465</fpage><lpage>472</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3711253</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><label>8</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nigrin</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><source><italic>Neural Networks for Pattern Recognition</italic></source><year>1993</year><publisher-loc>Cambridge, Mass, USA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>MIT Press</publisher-name><series>Bradford Books</series></citation></ref><ref id="B6"><label>9</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hebb</surname><given-names>DO</given-names></name></person-group><source><italic>The Organization of Behavior</italic></source><year>1949</year><publisher-loc>New York, NY, USA</publisher-loc><publisher-name>John Wiley &#x00026; Sons</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><label>10</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Paulsen</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Sejnowski</surname><given-names>TJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Natural patterns of activity and long-term synaptic plasticity</article-title><source><italic>Current Opinion in Neurobiology</italic></source><year>2000</year><volume>10</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>172</fpage><lpage>179</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10753798</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B5"><label>11</label><citation citation-type="confproc"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Harford</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Automatic segmentation, learning and retrieval of melodies using a self-organizing neural network</article-title><conf-name>In: Proceedings of the 4th International Conference on Music Information Retrieval (ISMIR '03)</conf-name><conf-date>October 2003</conf-date><conf-loc>Baltimore, Md, USA</conf-loc></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><label>12</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Marshall</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Gupta</surname><given-names>VS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Generalization and exclusive allocation of creditin unsupervised category learning</article-title><source><italic>Network: Computation in Neural Systems</italic></source><year>1998</year><volume>9</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>279</fpage><lpage>302</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><label>13</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Spratling</surname><given-names>MW</given-names></name><name><surname>Johnson</surname><given-names>MH</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Neural coding strategies and mechanisms of competition</article-title><source><italic>Cognitive Systems Research</italic></source><year>2004</year><volume>5</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>93</fpage><lpage>117</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Scutt</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Lancaster</surname><given-names>GA</given-names></name><name><surname>Manning</surname><given-names>JT</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Breast asymmetry and predisposition to breast cancer</article-title><source><italic>Breast Cancer Research</italic></source><year>2006</year><volume>8</volume><issue>2, article R14</issue></citation></ref><ref id="B7"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="confproc"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Howard</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Roberts</surname><given-names>SC</given-names></name><name><surname>Ryan</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Machine vision: exploring context with genetic programming</article-title><conf-name>In: Proceedings of the Genetic and Evolutionary Computation Conference (GECCO '02)</conf-name><conf-date>July 2002</conf-date><conf-loc>New York, NY, USA</conf-loc><publisher-name>Morgan Kaufmann</publisher-name><fpage>756</fpage><lpage>763</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B8"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Howard</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Roberts</surname><given-names>SC</given-names></name><name><surname>Ryan</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Pragmatic genetic programming strategy for the problem of vehicle detection in airborne reconnaissance</article-title><source><italic>Pattern Recognition Letters</italic></source><year>2006</year><volume>27</volume><issue>11</issue><fpage>1275</fpage><lpage>1288</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Longitudinal study showing the process of involution of the parenchyma with age of subject(digitized from L. Tab&#x000e1;r archive). The CC view is shown from examinations in1980, 1989, 1997, and 2002 (left to right).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Left to right: longitudinal progression of a distortion (digitized from L. Tab&#x000e1;rarchive).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Left to right: longitudinal progression of a distortion (digitized from L. Tab&#x000e1;r archive).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.003"/></fig><fig id="fig4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Left to right: longitudinal progression of a distortion (digitized from L. Tab&#x000e1;r archive).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.004"/></fig><fig id="fig5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>SONNET architecture.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.005"/></fig><fig id="fig6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Mammogram regions.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.006"/></fig><fig id="fig7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>Average and maximum correlations between mammogram features against the number of features omitted.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.007"/></fig><fig id="fig8" position="float"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p>Average normalized distance between mammograms against the number of featuresomitted.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.008"/></fig><fig id="fig9" position="float"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p>Maximum normalized distance between mammograms against the number of features omitted.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.009"/></fig><fig id="fig10" position="float"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p>Comparing the distances between mammograms using 53 features: (a) <italic>same</italic>, the left and right views withinpatients, (b) diff D1, the right views between patients, and (c) diffS1, the left views between patients.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.010"/></fig><fig id="fig11" position="float"><label>Figure 11</label><caption><p>The number of classes formed on the best SONNET epochs. <italic>nClass</italic> is the total number of classesformed and <italic>nStable</italic> is the numberof stable classes. The epochs are ranked according to descending <italic>nClass</italic> and then descending <italic>nStable</italic>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.011"/></fig><fig id="fig12" position="float"><label>Figure 12</label><caption><p>Average distance in input feature space for between-class mammograms and within-classmammograms for the best SONNET epochs. The ratio of between-class distance overwithin-class distance is also shown. The epoch ranking corresponds to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig11">Figure 11</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.012"/></fig><fig id="fig13" position="float"><label>Figure 13</label><caption><p>The fraction ofpatients whose two mammograms received the same classification for the bestSONNET epochs. The epoch ranking corresponds to <xref ref-type="fig" rid="fig11">Figure 11</xref>.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.013"/></fig><fig id="fig14" position="float"><label>Figure 14</label><caption><p>Example of a taxonomic class.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.014"/></fig><fig id="fig15" position="float"><label>Figure 15</label><caption><p>Example of a taxonomic class.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.015"/></fig><fig id="fig16" position="float"><label>Figure 16</label><caption><p>Example of a taxonomic class.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.016"/></fig><fig id="fig17" position="float"><label>Figure 17</label><caption><p>Example of a taxonomic class.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JBB2008-526343.017"/></fig><fig id="fig18" position="float"><label>Figure 18</label><caption><p>Example of a taxonomic class.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="JB